Is it a withdrawal syndrome?

257
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:31,159
That's a really insightful question.

258
00:10:31,159 --> 00:10:32,879
It's important to clarify, though.

259
00:10:32,879 --> 00:10:36,840
It's not considered a classic drug withdrawal syndrome, like you might see with opioids or

260
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:40,000
alcohol, with specific physical withdrawal symptoms.

261
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:41,919
It's more of a physiological rebound.

262
00:10:41,919 --> 00:10:46,039
The drug was actively suppressing your appetite and altering your metabolism.

263
00:10:46,039 --> 00:10:47,039
Yeah.

264
00:10:47,039 --> 00:10:49,000
And you remove it, those effects vanish.

265
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,159
So your hunger just comes roaring back?

266
00:10:51,159 --> 00:10:52,159
Pretty much.

267
00:10:52,199 --> 00:10:55,759
Your body's natural hunger signals kind of fire up again.

268
00:10:55,759 --> 00:11:00,559
Many people report feeling much hungerier, having more intense cravings, thinking about

269
00:11:00,559 --> 00:11:02,360
food more.

270
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:07,719
And alongside that, those metabolic benefits, the better blood pressure, the improved cholesterol,

271
00:11:07,719 --> 00:11:11,480
lower inflammation, those tend to reverse too.

272
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:15,759
And for people with diabetes, obviously, their blood sugar control will worsen if they

273
00:11:15,759 --> 00:11:17,799
don't have another treatment in place.

274
00:11:17,799 --> 00:11:20,599
So the key takeaway is, these aren't really a cure.

275
00:11:20,599 --> 00:11:22,639
They're an ongoing treatment.

276
00:11:22,639 --> 00:11:25,519
Expecting the results to stick after stopping is unrealistic.

277
00:11:25,519 --> 00:11:28,079
For most people, yes, that's the reality.

